<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guipuzcoan Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> was a Basque trading company chartered by the Spanish Crown in 1728 with a monopoly on trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the province of Venezuela. It shipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> to Spain, fought smuggling and built ports and forts, but its low prices for cacao angered planters and provoked the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-francisco-de-leon">Juan Francisco de León</a> in 1749. It lost its monopoly and was merged into the Royal Company of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1785.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guipuzcoan-company-of-caracas.jpg" alt="Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas" width="165" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Compania Guipuzcoana Madrid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guipuzcoan_Company_Caracas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
